<section class="ad2e-manual-layout">
  <nav class="ad2e-manual-nav">
    {{#each sections as |s|}}
    <button type="button" data-action="section" data-section="{{s.id}}" class="{{#if s.active}}active{{/if}}">{{s.label}}</button>
    {{/each}}
    <p class="ad2e-note">{{localize "AD2E.Manual.Version"}} {{version}}</p>
  </nav>
  <div class="ad2e-manual-body">

  {{#if is.start}}
  <h2>Getting started</h2>
  <p>This system runs the mechanics of AD&amp;D 2nd Edition: characters, monsters, combat, spells, treasure and the optional
  rules listed under <em>Optional rules</em>. It holds no rulebook text: item sheets link to the source page instead, so keep
  the books (or the AD&amp;D 2e wiki) at hand.</p>
  <h3>First steps for a new world</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Check the world settings (<em>Settings</em> section): death rule, initiative method, encumbrance rule and the optional rules.</li>
    <li>Import the monsters you need (<em>GM tools</em> &gt; Import monsters). Their pictures are copied into the world folder <code>ad2e-monsters</code>.</li>
    <li>Import spells beyond the 12 examples (<em>GM tools</em> &gt; Import spells); they go into the world compendium "Imported Spells".</li>
    <li>Create characters: drag a race, then a class, then a kit from the compendiums onto the sheet (<em>Characters</em> section).</li>
    <li>After a system update that changes imported data, run <em>Update existing monsters</em> and <em>Update existing spells</em>.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>All of the system's windows and dialogs can be resized; dialogs taller than the screen are fitted to it and scroll,
  so their buttons stay reachable.</p>
  <h3>Compendiums of this system</h3>
  <ul class="ad2e-manual-columns">
    {{#each packs as |p|}}<li>{{p.label}} <span class="ad2e-weapon-meta">({{p.type}})</span></li>{{/each}}
  </ul>
  {{/if}}

  {{#if is.tools}}
  <h2>GM tools</h2>
  {{#unless isGM}}<p class="ad2e-unmet">{{localize "AD2E.Manual.GmOnly"}}</p>{{/unless}}
  <h3>Creating</h3>
  <div class="ad2e-manual-tools">
    <div class="ad2e-manual-tool"><button type="button" data-action="tool" data-tool="quickNpc"><i class="fa-solid fa-bolt"></i> Quick NPC</button>
      <p>One click: a random NPC card in chat (whispered to you), made into an actor only if you press "Create actor".</p>
      <details><summary>Details</summary>
      <p>Rolls an NPC with the Random NPC rules, every choice random (townsfolk by default), and whispers you a card: race,
      class and level, gender, age, alignment, ability scores, hit points, AC, THAC0 and saving throws (as the character sheet
      would work them out from the class's basic gear), role, height and weight, personality and looks. Nothing is created:
      press "Create actor" on the card to make the character actor (in the world; its sheet opens), or pick a purpose beside
      "Another" and roll a new card. For a macro: <code>game.ad2e.quickNpc("soldier")</code> (any purpose: townsfolk,
      soldier, adventurer, official, sage, caster, spy, assassin).</p></details></div>
    <div class="ad2e-manual-tool"><button type="button" data-action="tool" data-tool="createNpc"><i class="fa-solid fa-user-plus"></i> Random NPC</button>
      <p>A random NPC by purpose with every choice open; creates a character actor.</p>
      <details><summary>Details</summary>
      <p>Pick a purpose (townsfolk, soldier, adventurer, official, sage, spellcaster for hire, spy, assassin) and, if you
      like, the race, class, level (a number or dice such as 1d4), gender (male, female, non-binary or agender),
      the height and weight column (PHB Table 10 male or female base figures; "Automatic" follows a male or female
      gender and rolls either for the others), profession, troop type, title culture or sage
      field; anything left on "Random" is rolled. "Roll" shows the NPC; "Create" makes a character actor with race and
      class items, abilities (3d6 in order, raised to the race's and class's minimums), hit points, basic equipment for
      the class (a soldier's from their troop type, a tradesman's proficiency), age, height and weight, personality traits
      and looks; the notes on the Character tab list the rest (title, sage field and ability, wage, base morale, spell costs for
      casters). Spells are not chosen: add them on the sheet. 0-level NPCs have no class.</p></details></div>
    <div class="ad2e-manual-tool"><button type="button" data-action="tool" data-tool="createPatron"><i class="fa-solid fa-crown"></i> Create patron</button>
      <p>An NPC who hires or sponsors the party, with DMG personality and looks.</p>
      <details><summary>Details</summary>
      <p>An NPC who hires or sponsors the party, as a Monster / NPC actor with the role "Patron". Choose race, class group
      and level (THAC0, saving throws and hit points follow; 0-level is possible), alignment, AC and morale; write what the
      patron wants, offers and pays; choose or roll a personality from DMG Table 70 and appearance words from the DMG's NPC
      section. Everything but the numbers goes into the actor's notes.</p></details></div>
    <div class="ad2e-manual-tool"><button type="button" data-action="tool" data-tool="createMonster"><i class="fa-solid fa-dragon"></i> Create monster</button>
      <p>A new Monster / NPC actor; THAC0, saves and XP follow the Hit Dice.</p>
      <details><summary>Details</summary>
      <p>A guided window for a new Monster / NPC actor: size, intelligence and morale with their Monstrous Manual bands,
      Hit Dice, AC, movement and attacks. THAC0 and saving throws follow the Hit Dice; experience is DMG Table 31 for the
      Hit Dice plus the Table 32 special abilities you tick (you may enter your own value). Hit points are rolled. The
      actor goes into the world or an unlocked world compendium, and its sheet opens for anything else.</p></details></div>
    <div class="ad2e-manual-tool"><button type="button" data-action="tool" data-tool="createWeapon"><i class="fa-solid fa-khanda"></i> Create weapon</button>
      <p>A new weapon item, from scratch or copied from another, with a matching proficiency.</p>
      <details><summary>Details</summary>
      <p>A guided window for a new weapon item: start from any weapon in the compendiums or the Items directory, or a blank
      one, and set its size, type, speed factor, damage (two rows, e.g. one- and two-handed), melee and missile use, rate
      of fire and range, Strength use, magical bonuses and notes; these statistics are read-only on the weapon sheet. It can
      create a matching weapon proficiency, or link an existing one so characters proficient with it use it (untick "New
      proficiency" for the list). A weapon's sheet has the same list as its "Weapon proficiency" drop-down.</p></details></div>
    <div class="ad2e-manual-tool"><button type="button" data-action="tool" data-tool="createItem"><i class="fa-solid fa-sack-dollar"></i> Create item</button>
      <p>Equipment, a container, armour or ammunition.</p>
      <details><summary>Details</summary>
      <p>A guided window for equipment (with its category), a container (capacity in pounds or volume), armour (body armour
      AC, a shield's bonus against melee and missiles and the attackers it covers, a helmet; the size it was made for and a
      magical bonus) or ammunition (the weapons that fire it and its damage). Start from an existing item of that kind or a
      blank one; the cost is checked ("5 gp", "2 sp each").</p></details></div>
    <div class="ad2e-manual-tool"><button type="button" data-action="tool" data-tool="createMagicItem"><i class="fa-solid fa-hat-wizard"></i> Create magic item</button>
      <p>A magical item, or magical arms with a bonus, valued from the DMG tables.</p>
      <details><summary>Details</summary>
      <p>Either a magical item (category, charges when found for wands, rods and staves, rolled at once if you like, the
      classes that may use it, XP and gp values, a container's capacity and whether its contents weigh nothing), or
      magical arms: a copy of a weapon, armour or ammunition with a bonus (-5 to +5), named and valued from the DMG tables
      (armour +1 500 XP, swords +1 400 XP, other weapons +1 500 XP and so on; the value goes in the notes). Items are
      unidentified unless you tick Identified.</p></details></div>
    <div class="ad2e-manual-tool"><button type="button" data-action="tool" data-tool="createSpell"><i class="fa-solid fa-wand-sparkles"></i> Create spell</button>
      <p>A new wizard or priest spell, with the DMG research advice and check.</p>
      <details><summary>Details</summary>
      <p>A new wizard or priest spell: schools or spheres (the PHB lists, plus any typed), level, components, range, area,
      casting time, duration, save and damage or healing options (rolled from the Spells tab). Advice from the DMG's
      spell research section: the level for the damage dice, research time (two weeks per level, a week more per failed
      check) and cost (100-1,000 gp per level). Choose the researching character to roll the research check (a wizard's
      chance to learn the spell, a priest's Wisdom check); once researched the spell can go straight into their spells.</p></details></div>
    <div class="ad2e-manual-tool"><button type="button" data-action="tool" data-tool="createTrap"><i class="fa-solid fa-burst"></i> Create trap</button>
      <p>A trap actor or trap item with its attack or save, damage and a Spring button.</p>
      <details><summary>Details</summary>
      <p>A trap actor (a token on the map: pits, deadfalls) or a trap item (put on the chest, door or other object it
      guards). It hits by an attack at its THAC0 against the victim's AC, or by the victim's saving throw (avoided or halved
      on a success); damage, trigger, effect and reset; and a find/remove modifier up to 30% either way. The trap's sheet
      (or the item's) has a GM "Spring" button: target the victims' tokens first; the attack or saves are rolled and the
      damage comes with the usual apply buttons. A thief's Find/Remove Traps roll offers the targeted trap's modifier and a
      silent attempt (-10%, a click on 01-10).</p></details></div>
  </div>
  <h3>At the table</h3>
  <div class="ad2e-manual-tools">
    <div class="ad2e-manual-tool"><button type="button" data-action="tool" data-tool="awardXp"><i class="fa-solid fa-star"></i> Award experience</button>
      <p>Shares an experience award among characters; opens by itself after a combat.</p>
      <details><summary>Details</summary>
      <p>Divides an experience award equally among the chosen characters; each share gets that character's prime requisite bonus. A single
      character's award is the "+" next to XP on the sheet. When you end a combat with monsters in it, this window opens by
      itself (world setting) with the dead monsters ticked and the combat's characters as recipients; tick monsters that
      fled or surrendered too. A monster with no XP value is calculated from its Hit Dice (marked "calculated"). Warriors
      (10 XP) and bards (5 XP) can also get the DMG's class award per Hit Die of the creatures defeated: untick it for
      anyone who did not earn it.</p></details></div>
    <div class="ad2e-manual-tool"><button type="button" data-action="tool" data-tool="rollTreasure"><i class="fa-solid fa-coins"></i> Roll treasure</button>
      <p>Rolls DMG treasure types to a GM card that adds them to a token's actor.</p>
      <details><summary>Details</summary>
      <p>Rolls DMG treasure types (e.g. "Q x5, X"): coins, gems, objects of art and magical items. The result is a GM chat card;
      its button adds everything to the selected token's actor, magical items unidentified. Monster sheets have the same
      button, prefilled from the stat block.</p></details></div>
    <div class="ad2e-manual-tool"><button type="button" data-action="tool" data-tool="rollReaction"><i class="fa-solid fa-comments"></i> Encounter reaction</button>
      <p>Rolls the encounter reaction table with the speaker's modifiers.</p>
      <details><summary>Details</summary>
      <p>Rolls the encounter reaction table with the speaker's Charisma and kit reaction modifiers. Monster sheets have the
      same button.</p></details></div>
    <div class="ad2e-manual-tool"><button type="button" data-action="tool" data-tool="encounters"><i class="fa-solid fa-dragon"></i> Random encounters</button>
      <p>Sets the encounter area, rolls checks (by hand or as time passes) and builds encounter tables.</p>
      <details><summary>Details</summary>
      <p>Set the area: wilderness (terrain and population: patrolled or sparsely settled +1, heavily populated +2) or a
      dungeon (every hour, or every turn in a dangerous part), an extra modifier, and the RollTable to use. "Check now"
      rolls 1d10 (an encounter on the chance or less); "Encounter now" skips the roll. With "Automatic" ticked, checks are
      rolled whenever world time passes (at the marked times of day outdoors, each hour or turn in a dungeon; not during a
      combat; they stop at the first encounter). The travel card's "End day" sets the area to the day's terrain and rolls
      that day's checks. The table builder takes creatures dragged in (or the selected tokens) and text entries, each
      with a frequency (read from the stat block; unique creatures are refused), and creates a RollTable in the 2-20
      layout (1d8+1d12) or the percentile layout; a table level lowers the frequency of creatures of other levels. An
      encounter card is whispered to you, with the creature, the number appearing, and buttons for the distance, surprise
      (the creature, or each selected token) and the reaction roll.</p></details></div>
    <div class="ad2e-manual-tool"><button type="button" data-action="tool" data-tool="poisonTargets"><i class="fa-solid fa-skull-crossbones"></i> Poison targets</button>
      <p>Gives a DMG poison to the targeted tokens and asks for their saves.</p>
      <details><summary>Details</summary>
      <p>Gives a poison (DMG Table 51 class A-P) to the targeted tokens: a drugged drink, a contact poison on a door handle.
      Choose how it is given: a poison given another way than its own works at half strength (injected or ingested
      swapped), and injected or ingested poisons do nothing by contact. A save request goes to chat (see Combat, Poison).</p></details></div>
    <div class="ad2e-manual-tool"><button type="button" data-action="tool" data-tool="falling"><i class="fa-solid fa-person-falling"></i> Falling damage</button>
      <p>Falling damage for the targeted tokens, with item saves if wanted.</p>
      <details><summary>Details</summary>
      <p>Target (or select) the tokens that fell and enter the distance: 1d6 per full 10 feet, at most 20d6, posted with the
      usual apply buttons (no massive-damage check: a fall is not an attack). Item saves vs. Fall follow if ticked (more
      than 5 feet; soft surface +5, -1 per 5 feet beyond the first).</p></details></div>
    <div class="ad2e-manual-tool"><button type="button" data-action="tool" data-tool="itemSaves"><i class="fa-solid fa-flask"></i> Item saving throws</button>
      <p>DMG Table 29 item saving throws for the selected tokens' items.</p>
      <details><summary>Details</summary>
      <p>For the selected (or targeted) tokens: choose the attack form (acid, crushing blow, disintegration, fall, magical
      or normal fire, cold, lightning bolt, electricity) and roll DMG Table 29 for the ticked items. Fragile items
      (potions, oils, paper, glass, pottery) are ticked; adjust material, bonus and the +2 "designed to counter" box per item.
      A failed item is renamed "(destroyed)", unequipped and no longer carried; delete it when ready. The same window opens
      from the "Item saves" button next to a failed save on a spell or poison save card.</p></details></div>
  </div>
  <h3>Party, travel and time</h3>
  <div class="ad2e-manual-tools">
    <div class="ad2e-manual-tool"><button type="button" data-action="tool" data-tool="party"><i class="fa-solid fa-people-group"></i> Party</button>
      <p>Who is in the party, used by travel, dungeon turns, experience and reactions.</p>
      <details><summary>Details</summary>
      <p>List who is in the party: drag actors from the Actors sidebar onto the window, or use "Add selected tokens" or
      "Add player characters"; the arrows reorder the list and the cross removes a member (a deleted actor leaves it by
      itself). Each row shows hit points and the henchmen, animals and mount that come along. The travel planner starts
      with the party ("Use party" brings it back), the dungeon turn fatigue penalty applies to the party's characters, and
      the group experience award (outside combat) and reaction rolls offer the party's characters. While the list is
      empty every player-owned character counts as the party, as before.</p></details></div>
    <div class="ad2e-manual-tool"><button type="button" data-action="tool" data-tool="travel"><i class="fa-solid fa-route"></i> Weather and travel</button>
      <p>Daily weather, travel planning, supplies, travel events and the day log.</p>
      <details><summary>Details</summary>
      <p>Pick the season and roll the day's weather (2d6 wind by season, adverse winds, rain or snow); it is kept for the
      world and can be changed by hand. The planner starts with the party from the Party window (else the selected tokens,
      else the player characters); "Use selected tokens" or "Use party" changes it: the slowest member sets the pace (a rider uses the
      mount's rate). Choose on foot (normal or force march), mounted, vehicle, boat, ship or flying, and add the day's
      terrain legs with trail or road and obstacles (the weather ticks its own); leave the last leg's miles empty to
      spend the rest of the day there. "Post the day" writes the card with the miles, rolls force-march checks
      (characters Constitution, creatures save vs. death; a failure stops force marching) and a blind getting-lost
      check for you. The card's "End day" button advances world time by the travel hours. "Rest" removes force-march
      penalty days (-1 to attack rolls per day; half a day of rest per day).</p>
      <p>Supplies, events and the day log: when "Characters eat and drink at End day" is ticked, End day takes a day of
      rations and the water per day (½ gallon unless you change it; 1 gallon is the Spelljammer rule) from each character
      in the party, emptiest waterskin first; the table shows what each has left and any hunger or thirst. Without food
      or water the Spelljammer (Complete Spacefarer's Handbook) rules apply: Constitution checks, lost Strength and
      Constitution (shown on the sheet), a save vs. death at Constitution 0, death after a week without water; the
      losses come back once fed and watered. Henchmen, animals and the mount of each character come along: animals eat
      "Animal feed (per day)" (or tick "Animals graze today") and drink the amount for their size from the "Animal water per day
      by size" row (empty boxes use the defaults in grey: about 10 gallons for a horse, 0.5 for a dog, 50 for an elephant;
      real-world figures, change them as you like), or none when you tick "Animals drink on the way today"; their feed
      is set the same way in the "Animal feed per day by size" row (units of Animal feed, 1 = a horse's day: a dog about
      0.05, an elephant about 5); anyone short eats from its employer's or rider's packs. Monsters' days without food or water are only counted
      for you. Living off the land: the Hunting, Fishing and Survival checks on the Skills &amp; Proficiencies tab add what
      they find as "Foraged food (per day)", eaten before carried rations. Hunting takes -1 per nonproficient hunter
      (counted from the party); a success gives the distance (100 + 1d100 yards) and, for you, buttons by the animal's
      size: after the kill, click one to add the Table 33 rations (Complete Barbarian's Handbook; more with Animal
      Rending). Fishing rolls once per hour asked: fish = Wisdom − roll, ×3 with a net, ×½ or ×2 for a poor or good spot,
      two fish a day of food. Survival looks for food or water once a day each, a failure ending the day's attempts; a
      success gives a day's food, or covers the finder's water on the next End day. Travel events: no events, the built-in list or a
      roll table of your own, with a 1-in-N chance each day; "Copy built-in list to a table" makes an editable table.
      Each End day writes a log entry (date, miles, weather, lost, encounter, event, supplies), listed in the planner
      and posted to you.</p></details></div>
    <div class="ad2e-manual-tool"><button type="button" data-action="tool" data-tool="dungeonTurns"><i class="fa-solid fa-hourglass-half"></i> Dungeon turns</button>
      <p>Ten-minute dungeon turns, rest reminders and burning lights.</p>
      <details><summary>Details</summary>
      <p>Start an expedition, then use "Next turn" and "Rest turn" (10 minutes of world time each; 6 turns are an hour).
      After 5 active turns you are whispered that the next turn should be a rest; every 6 turns without one give the
      party's characters (the Party window; the player characters while it is empty) -1 to attack rolls and checks until they rest. Torches, candles and lanterns lit on the Equipment
      tab burn down whenever world time passes (PHB Table 63: torch 3 turns, lantern 12 turns a flask of oil, candle a turn
      an inch), are used up or run dry with a chat notice, and light their bearer's token; the tracker lists them with
      their turns left and can put them out.</p></details></div>
    <div class="ad2e-manual-tool"><button type="button" data-action="tool" data-tool="calendar"><i class="fa-solid fa-moon"></i> Zakharan calendar</button>
      <p>The Zakharan (Al-Qadim) calendar: date, holidays and constellations.</p>
      <details><summary>Details</summary>
      <p>For Al-Qadim games: turn on "Zakharan (Al-Qadim) calendar" in Configure Settings (the world reloads). The world's
      date then follows the Zakharan year: Taraq, Masta, Magarib, Gammam, Mihla, Qawafil, the five High Holy Days (Ahad,
      Atnen, Salas, Arba, Yasad), Safa, Dar, Riyah, Nau, Rahat, Saris; 30-day months, 365 days. The window shows today's
      date with any holiday of Golden Huzuz, the constellations fully visible this month (the sha'ir astrologer's list) and
      the year's months; set the year, month and day there (world time moves to that day, keeping the time of day; timers
      such as lights, poisons and travel see the time pass, so set the date before the adventure starts). The travel log
      writes Zakharan dates, the dungeon turn tracker shows the date and time of day, and the travel planner starts in the date's season: spring from 19 Magarib, summer from 20
      Qawafil (the High Holy Days are in summer), fall from 21 Riyah, winter from 20 Saris (the Harptos equinoxes and
      solstices on the paired Zakharan months; the season select in the planner still overrides it for the day). Players can open the window from Configure Settings. Not in the sources, chosen here: no leap
      day, years counted from 0 (set the year you play in) and a ten-day week of numbered days.</p></details></div>
  </div>
  <h3>Importing</h3>
  <div class="ad2e-manual-tools">
    <div class="ad2e-manual-tool"><button type="button" data-action="tool" data-tool="importMonsters"><i class="fa-solid fa-dragon"></i> Import monsters</button>
      <p>Monster / NPC actors from completecompendium.com stat blocks, with picture and token size.</p>
      <details><summary>Details</summary>
      <p>Choose a setting and source book from completecompendium.com, select stat blocks and import them as Monster / NPC
      actors (stat block, link to the page, picture as portrait and token; token size from the size letter: T ½, S and M 1,
      L 2, H 3, G 4 squares). Importing the same stat block again updates the actor.</p></details></div>
    <div class="ad2e-manual-tool"><button type="button" data-action="tool" data-tool="updateMonsters"><i class="fa-solid fa-rotate"></i> Update existing monsters</button>
      <p>Re-reads the page of every imported monster; your own changes stay.</p>
      <details><summary>Details</summary>
      <p>Re-reads the page of every imported monster in the world and in unlocked world compendiums. Names, current hit
      points, pictures and token sizes you set, and attack elements you chose stay; tokens already placed on scenes get the
      new picture and size if they still had the old one.</p></details></div>
    <div class="ad2e-manual-tool"><button type="button" data-action="tool" data-tool="importSpells"><i class="fa-solid fa-wand-sparkles"></i> Import spells</button>
      <p>Spells from the AD&amp;D 2e wiki into the world compendium "Imported Spells".</p>
      <details><summary>Details</summary>
      <p>Choose a source book from the AD&amp;D 2e wiki, filter by class and level, and import the spells' statistics and links
      into the world compendium "Imported Spells" (folders by class and level). Material components are linked to the Spell
      Components compendium, and damage or healing dice are read for the spell's Damage button. After each import the
      compendium is read back from the server: if it is missing or lacks any imported spell, an error replaces the success
      message (details in the browser console, F12).</p></details></div>
    <div class="ad2e-manual-tool"><button type="button" data-action="tool" data-tool="updateSpells"><i class="fa-solid fa-rotate"></i> Update existing spells</button>
      <p>Refreshes spells from their wiki pages; memorization and notes stay.</p>
      <details><summary>Details</summary>
      <p>Refreshes every spell in the Items directory, on characters and in "Imported Spells" from its wiki page; memorization,
      learned status and notes stay.</p></details></div>
  </div>
  <h3>Maintenance</h3>
  <div class="ad2e-manual-tools">
    <div class="ad2e-manual-tool"><button type="button" data-action="tool" data-tool="selfCheck"><i class="fa-solid fa-stethoscope"></i> Check sheets</button>
      <p>Opens sample sheets and windows and lists display faults and errors, for the system's maintainer.</p>
      <details><summary>Details</summary>
      <p>Opens sample sheets (up to 6 characters, up to 3 monsters, one item of each type) and the system's windows, shows
      every tab (characters also in edit mode) and lists what looks wrong: labels shown as raw keys (<code>AD2E.…</code>), unfilled
      <code>{…}</code>, <code>undefined</code> or <code>NaN</code>, fields squeezed narrower than 30 pixels, text running out of its box or wrapped one word
      per line, items covering each other, and errors logged while drawing. Nothing is changed; windows it opened are closed
      again. Copy the report and send it to whoever maintains the system. Not covered: dialogs and the importers.</p></details></div>
    <div class="ad2e-manual-tool"><button type="button" data-action="tool" data-tool="settings"><i class="fa-solid fa-gears"></i> Configure Settings</button>
      <p>Foundry's settings window; this system's settings are under its own tab.</p>
      <details><summary>Details</summary>
      <p>Foundry's settings window; this system's settings are under its own tab.</p></details></div>
  </div>
  {{/if}}

  {{#if is.settings}}
  <h2>Settings</h2>
  <p>Current values in this world. World settings apply to everyone and only the GM changes them; client settings are per
  user and browser. Change them in Configure Settings.</p>
  <p><button type="button" data-action="tool" data-tool="settings"><i class="fa-solid fa-gears"></i> Configure Settings</button></p>
  <table class="ad2e-manual-table">
    <thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Value</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
    {{#each settings as |s|}}
    <tr><td><strong>{{s.name}}</strong><br><span class="ad2e-weapon-meta">{{s.scope}}{{#if s.reload}}, {{localize "AD2E.Manual.Reload"}}{{/if}}</span></td>
      <td>{{s.value}}</td><td>{{s.hint}}</td></tr>
    {{/each}}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <h3>Setting menus</h3>
  <ul>
    {{#each menus as |m|}}
    <li><button type="button" data-action="menu" data-menu="{{m.key}}"><i class="{{m.icon}}"></i> {{m.name}}</button> {{m.hint}}</li>
    {{/each}}
  </ul>
  {{/if}}

  {{#if is.characters}}
  <h2>Characters</h2>
  <h3>The character sheet</h3>
  <p>Six tabs, each control in one place: Main (abilities with their checks and tests, saving throws, health, Armour
  Class and THAC0; the movement figures and jumps are in the footer of every tab), Combat (attacks and combat actions, weapons in hand), Inventory, Spells (casters only),
  Skills &amp; Proficiencies (class skills and features, proficiencies, languages, traits) and Character (race, class and
  kit, personal details, station, familiar, animals, henchmen, followers, biography). The Edit button in the header
  switches between play mode and edit mode: rolled scores, maximum hit points, base AC, THAC0 and save overrides,
  initiative and magic resistance, level, experience, alignment, kit choices, skill points, languages, personal details
  and the bin icons appear only while editing (a new character without race or class opens in edit mode; the mode is
  kept per open sheet, not saved). The <strong>GM</strong> section at the end of the Character tab is shown only to GMs:
  energy drain and restore, the dual-class buttons, Find Familiar, the bonded animal's table and loss buttons, attracting
  followers, the station controls (initial station, convict, penniless and debts, enslave) and the maximum age. The
  construction start/stop and the gen's "Return now" stay beside their panels (GM only there too).</p>
  <h3>Creating a character</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Enter the rolled ability scores on the Abilities panel, or use its dice button (PHB Methods II-VI; exceptional
    Strength for warriors with 18).</li>
    <li>Drag a race from "Races (PHB)". Racial adjustments are added to the rolled scores; the race limits the classes.</li>
    <li>Drag a class from "Classes (PHB)". Minimums, alignment and racial level limits are checked on the Class tab.</li>
    <li>Optionally drag a kit from "Class Kits". A kit that does not fit the class or race is refused or flagged. Kit bonus
    proficiencies are added free; required ones use slots. Kit modifiers apply automatically, or as tick boxes in the roll
    dialogs when they depend on the situation.</li>
    <li>Drag proficiencies from "Proficiencies". Slots used and available are shown; extra slots on a nonweapon proficiency
    add +1 each. Traits and disadvantages (Skills &amp; Powers) are optional; traits must be paid for with disadvantages.</li>
    <li>Equip weapons, armour and gear from the equipment compendiums (Equipped tick box). Coins are items; containers carry items.</li>
    <li>Spellcasters: drop spells on the sheet. Wizards and bards roll to learn; the Spells tab shows slots by level, Memorize
    (-/+), Cast and Rest. The Spells tab is hidden for characters without spells.</li>
  </ol>
  <h3>Age</h3>
  <p>On the Character tab, "Roll" rolls the starting age (PHB Table 11: base + dice for the race); your own field "Maximum age
  (GM)" and its Roll button are in the GM section (hidden from players), and you are whispered when the character reaches it (elves leave
  rather than die). The ageing changes of PHB Table 12 (middle age, old, venerable) are applied to the scores from the Age
  and race, cumulatively; exceptional Strength is halved at middle age and lost (with 1 point) when old. Magical
  longevity changes the maximum age, not the age.</p>
  <h3>Languages</h3>
  <p>Four world settings decide how languages work: counting (proficiency slots: Intelligence Table 4 adds nonweapon slots
  and each modern language beyond the free ones takes a Languages, Modern slot; or Table 4: that many additional
  languages free, with no extra nonweapon slots; reload after changing it), when they are chosen (at the start by the
  player, or learned in play: then only you can add or remove them on the sheet), the common language every character
  speaks free (e.g. Common, or Midani for Al-Qadim; empty for none) and your campaign's language list (comma-separated;
  offered on the sheet with the race's initial languages and Midani). The Languages section on the Skills &amp; Proficiencies tab shows
  the counts against their limits in red when over, flags a modern language that is not in the race's or campaign's list
  (allowed if you agree) and ignores a repeated one. Ancient languages take Languages, Ancient slots and are either read
  and written (ticked) or spoken; literacy in other languages takes Reading/Writing slots, one language each.</p>
  <h3>Advancing</h3>
  <p>"Level Up" rolls hit points; saving throws, THAC0, slots, thief skills and spells follow the level. Thief and bard skill
  points are spent on the Skills &amp; Proficiencies tab. Henchmen: drag an actor onto the sheet (Character tab). Mounts, pack animals and
  pets: drag the animal actor onto the sheet; they are listed on the Inventory tab with their load, and "Riding" marks the
  one the character rides (its load then includes the rider).</p>
  <h3>One level per award (optional)</h3>
  <p>World setting "One level per experience award", off by default. When on, an award (the + on the sheet, Award
  experience, the award after a combat) never takes a character past one point below the experience for two levels up;
  the rest is lost and the chat message says how much. Multi-class characters are capped per class. Source: Rate of
  Advancement (DMG), which lets you place the character anywhere from halfway to one point below the next level: the
  system uses one point below; lower the experience by hand if you want less.</p>
  <h3>Training for level advancement (optional)</h3>
  <p>World setting "Training for level advancement", off by default. When on, Level Up is refused until the character has
  finished training for that level; you can still level a character up without it (Level Up asks you to confirm). The
  Character tab gets a Training panel:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Start training</strong>: choose the tutor. The list shows characters in the world of the same class and a
    higher level than the level trained for (a 7th-level fighter training for 8th needs a 9th-level or higher fighter), or
    "another tutor" with a name, level and Wisdom. A tutor owned by a player rolls a Wisdom and a Charisma check once (kept
    on that character; both must succeed to teach); other tutors pass automatically, and you can tick "Skip the tutor's
    checks". The tutor must also know what is taught (the specialization, the spell, the skill): that is up to you.</li>
    <li><strong>Fee</strong>: the rule sets no amount; the dialog suggests 100 gp per level per week (0 = free). The minimum
    weeks are paid from the character's coins when training starts, one more week after each failed check.</li>
    <li><strong>Time</strong>: 19 minus the tutor's Wisdom weeks (at least 1; a week is 7 days of game time). Then
    <strong>Training check</strong>: Intelligence or Wisdom, whichever is higher; a failure adds a week and +1 to the next
    check. A success unlocks Level Up for that level.</li>
    <li>Multi-class characters train one class at a time (the dialog asks which). Stop training refunds nothing.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Source: Training (Optional Rule) (DMG). Implementation choices: the fee default, payment by week, at least one week,
  7-day weeks, the tutor's checks kept.</p>
  <h3>Followers and strongholds</h3>
  <p>On the Character tab, "Followers &amp; stronghold" records the stronghold (name, kind, Built). Each class with followers shows
  the level it needs: fighter 9 and bard 9 (a built stronghold), cleric 8 (a built place of worship), ranger 10 and thief
  10. When a class is ready, the "Attract followers" button in the GM section rolls once (Table 16, 19 or 31, or the number of
  0-level soldiers; cleric 2d10 x 10), creates one Monster / NPC actor per unit (role "Follower unit", "Figures in unit"
  = how many it stands for, hit points of one figure) and posts a GM-only card. Equipment without a compendium item and
  creatures without statistics are noted on the actor with a link. Units dropped on the sheet join the list; removing one
  keeps the actor. Paladins attract no followers.</p>
  <h3>Stronghold construction</h3>
  <p>On the Character tab, "Construction" (inside Followers &amp; stronghold) plans the castle: the site's production modifiers
  (worker skill and morale are yours to set), the tech level, the modules with count and style, hired workers (blank =
  the standard number) and helpers (selected tokens, the character's magical items with a suitability, or other help
  as a number of workers). The summary shows the cost, man/weeks, work weeks and calendar time. "Start construction"
  (GM) freezes the plan; work then accrues as world time passes, and each month an event is rolled and whispered to you
  with its effects applied and buttons for the choices. Problems to deal with in play (a monster, highwaymen, unrest)
  have a "Resolved" button on the card and on the sheet. When the work is done the stronghold is marked built.
  "Reset to planning" clears progress.</p>
  <h3>Dual-class characters</h3>
  <p>With the world setting "Dual-class characters" on, dropping a new class on a character who has one asks whether to
  dual-class or replace the class (dual-class only for eligible humans: prime requisites 15+ in the current class and 17+
  in the new one, at least 2nd level). The earlier class stays with its abilities and spells at its last level. Until the
  new class's level is higher, the earlier class's abilities, THAC0 or saves can still be used but set the experience
  penalty: experience awards then ask "encounter" (no experience) or "adventure" (half). The Character tab shows
  the status; the GM section has buttons to clear the penalty, and "Undo dual-class" to correct a mistaken switch: the current class
  is removed and the earlier one comes back from the Classes compendium at its last level, with the experience it had at
  the switch (for switches made before 0.0.122, the minimum for that level). Hit points and the kit are not changed.</p>
  <h3>Multi-class characters</h3>
  <p>With the world setting "Multi-class characters" on, dropping another class on a demihuman asks whether to add it
  (multi-class) or replace the class. Adding is offered at creation only, for the race's combinations, with an allowed
  alignment and the class's minimums. Experience awards (individual and group) are divided between the classes, each
  share with its class's prime requisite bonus. The Character tab lists the classes with a Level Up button each; a
  class stops at its racial level limit. After adding the classes, roll 1st-level hit points again (the dice are
  averaged). One kit, open to any of the classes; kits from the Complete Fighter's and Thief's Handbooks show a
  single-class warning for you to rule on. Bard combinations from the Complete Bard's Handbook need one of the kits the
  Character tab names. The other classes' levels and experience can be corrected in the class table. Weapons outside
  a class's list are marked (a priest's list always applies to a multi-class priest; for a specialty priest fill in
  "Allowed weapons" on the class or kit item); a multi-class druid's armour limits always show; casting a wizard spell in armour
  asks for confirmation (elves in elven chain excepted).</p>
  <h3>Class and kit weapons</h3>
  <p>The class items carry the PHB weapon lists (the cleric's bludgeoning rule is built in) and the kits carry the limits
  their pages give: their own list, extra weapons, forbidden weapons, weapons only allowed at 1st level, and notes for
  weapons that cost extra slots (not charged). A weapon outside them is marked on the weapon row and in the attack dialog;
  nothing is blocked. Edit them on the class or kit item (comma-separated names or identifiers; * = any weapon in
  "Allowed weapons", no weapon at all in "Forbidden weapons"); the Character tab shows the kit's limits. Class and
  kit copies made before version 1.0.27 were filled in once from the compendiums.</p>
  <h3>Skills &amp; Powers kit features</h3>
  <p>Social rank: Roll on the Character tab (the kit's 2d6 table). Encounter reactions ask the NPC's social class
  when a Pugilist is present and whether it is a first meeting with a Barbarian. A Weapon Master's "Display" (Combat
  tab, during combat) worsens the other side's initiative in rounds 1 and 2 (untick it in the dialog for anyone who did
  not see it). A Mystic's "Meditate" boosts one ability after the meditation for a third of its time; advance world time
  to start and end it. Weapon Masters: the chosen weapon is the melee weapon with specialization (else expertise), at no extra cost;
  proficiencies of another weapon type are marked. With the Skills &amp; Powers weapon rules the chosen weapon is also
  the weapon of choice (+1 to hit) at no cost, and the weapon-of-choice box is hidden.</p>
  <h3>Animal companions and mounts (Skills &amp; Powers)</h3>
  <p>Animal Masters and Riders have a bonded animal, shown on the Character tab. Roll Table 42 or 43 with the button
  there (or let the player choose); for a Rider choose the homeland (settled lands, desert, jungle, forest, mountains,
  underground, coast) so the mount suits it, or "Any" for the whole table and drag the creature from the chat card's link onto the sheet: the sheet offers to
  bond it. The creatures are in Hirelings &amp; Mounts, folder "Animal Companions &amp; Mounts (POSP)". When the animal
  dies, chat tells you; then use "Companion died" or "Companion lost carelessly" (-10% experience, that species never
  again), or "Mount died" (2d6 damage; for negligence a save vs. spells, on a failure the rider is feebleminded for the 2d6 hours: a status icon and a note on the Character tab,
  casting asks to confirm, it ends when world time passes or with "End feeblemind")
  and "Mount fled" (no bonded mount again). A companion larger than size S is marked on the Character tab (the kit asks
  for small animals); you decide whether it stays. Warnings (yours to decide) also appear on the table roll, the
  bonding prompt and the Character tab when a mount is too big (gnome, halfling) or too small (medium races) for the
  rider's race, when a companion is outside an Animal Master's race affinity (dwarf and gnome: burrowing animals; elf:
  forest animals), or when its good/evil alignment opposes the master's. Cavaliers and Nobles without a mount get a warning on the Character
  tab.</p>
  <h3>Energy drain and restoration</h3>
  <p>The GM section at the bottom of a character's Character tab (seen only by GMs) has two buttons for this. "Energy drain" asks how many levels are lost: each comes off the
  highest class (multi- and dual-class characters), reduces maximum hit points by that level's roll, and sets that
  class's experience halfway to the next level; wizard spells the character can no longer cast are marked not
  understood. If memorized spells exceed the new slots, a dialog asks which memorizations are forgotten (uncast ones
  are proposed). A character drained below 1st level becomes 0-level and cannot level up; a further drain kills the
  character, and you are whispered how many days (2d4) until the character rises as an undead of its slayer's type.
  "Restoration" brings back the most recent drained level (with its hit points and the minimum experience for the
  level) if cast in time: one day per level of the priest, which the dialog asks for; choose the casting priest there
  and both recorded ages (Character tab) go up by two years. Drained levels are listed on the Main tab until
  regained.</p>
  <h3>Hireling wages</h3>
  <p>A hireling (an NPC whose role is hireling) dragged onto a character's sheet as a henchman is paid from that
  character's coins every 30 days of game time, change included. The wage is read from the hireling's Cost field: the
  monthly figure ("2 gp per week, 10 gp per month" pays 10 gp), a weekly one ×30/7 when there is no monthly one, and the
  lower figure of a range (type the exact wage to change it). The first payment falls 30 days after the hireling is first
  seen with an employer. You and the employer's owner get a whisper listing who was paid; when the coins are short the
  wage is owed (shown on the hireling's sheet, with a Pay arrears button for you) and a morale check is suggested.</p>
  <h3>Spell effects</h3>
  <p>When a spell is cast (world setting "Spell effects at cast", on by default), a dialog shows its Duration entry with
  the length worked out at the casting level: per-level terms, ranges and dice (rolled) are read; a round is one minute
  and a turn ten minutes. Correct the length if needed, or leave it empty for no end (concentration, special and
  permanent spells start empty or with a suggestion). Choose who gets the effect: the targeted creatures (those that
  resisted by magic resistance are left out), the caster, or nobody. When a successful save negates the spell (its Save
  entry reads "Neg."), targeted creatures get the effect only when they fail: each one is created as its save is rolled,
  saves rolled before you answer the dialog included (a GM must be logged in). With a half or special save the effect is
  applied at once; end it with the cross beside it where your ruling says so. Instantaneous spells without modifiers ask
  nothing.</p>
  <p>Each recipient gets an Active Effect named after the spell, listed on the character's Main tab (Health) or the
  monster's header with the time left and an end button. Effects on creatures the caster's player does not own are
  created by you (a whispered card notes it; a GM must be logged in). When world time passes an effect's end it is
  removed and a whisper goes to you, the recipient's owners and the caster's owners; combat rounds advance world time
  by one minute each.</p>
  <p>Modifiers: on a spell's sheet, the "Spell effect" box has Attack, Damage, Armor Class, Saving throws and Checks
  fields that only GMs can change. Nothing is read from the spell's text: fill them in for spells such as Bless or
  Protection from Evil as you rule them. Armor Class +1 means one point better. While the effect lasts they are added to
  the recipient's attack and damage rolls (weapons and monster attacks), Armor Class (front, rear and missile), saving
  throws, and ability and proficiency checks; chat cards note "spell effects".</p>
  <h3>Spell animations (JB2A)</h3>
  <p>With the Sequencer module and a JB2A module (the Patreon "Complete Collection" or the free one) active, casting a spell
  plays an animation from the caster's token to the targeted tokens: missiles and bolts fly to each target, area spells
  burst on the first target, cones point at it, healing and blessings glow on the targets (or the caster when nothing is
  targeted). Spells without a fitting animation get a healing glow (if they heal) or a casting glow on the caster. The
  caster needs a token on the viewed scene. World setting "Spell animations (JB2A)" turns it off; without Sequencer or
  JB2A nothing is played and no error is shown. A spell's sheet shows the automatic choice under "Spell effect"; type
  another Sequencer database path (e.g. <code>jb2a.magic_missile.blue</code>) and how it is played to change it, or
  <code>none</code> for no animation.</p>
  <p>Weapon attacks (world setting "Weapon attack animations (JB2A)", on by default) play after the attack roll when a
  token is targeted: the weapon swings or flies from the attacker to each target (a miss lands beside it). Swords, axes,
  maces, flails, polearms, spears, daggers, bows, crossbows, slings, firearms, darts and thrown weapons have their own
  animations; other weapons get a slashing, piercing or bludgeoning swing by their damage type. Monster attacks named
  bite or claw show on the target (hits only); fists, slams, tails and tentacles strike; stings and gores pierce; "hurl
  rocks" throws a boulder. Unarmed attacks punch. Lasso, net and the attack options (disarm, grab and the like) play
  nothing.</p>
  <p>Each weapon's sheet has an "Animation and sound" section showing the automatic choices for melee and missile use.
  Type a Sequencer database path (e.g. <code>jb2a.spear.melee.01.white</code>) or a file path (e.g.
  <code>worlds/myworld/fx/blade.webm</code>; <code>*</code> picks one of several files at random) to change the animation,
  or <code>none</code> for no animation. The sound field works the same way (e.g. <code>psfx.weapon-attacks.spear.v1</code>);
  a chosen sound replaces both the swing and the impact, and <code>none</code> makes the weapon silent. A spell's
  animation field also accepts a file path. A monster's natural attacks have the same two fields on the line under each
  attack in its Attacks table; an empty field shows the automatic choice in grey.</p>
  <h3>Sound effects (PSFX)</h3>
  <p>With the Sequencer module and Peri's Sound Effects (PSFX) active, casting a spell and rolling an attack play a sound
  for everyone (world settings "Sound effects (PSFX)", on by default, and "Sound effects volume"). Spells use the sound
  that fits the animation chosen for them (Magic Missile, Fireball's flight and blast, Lightning Bolt, Cone of Cold,
  Burning Hands, Cure Wounds, Bless, Shield, Sleep, Web, Dimension Door and others), otherwise a generic casting sound;
  a spell whose animation is set to <code>none</code> is silent. Attacks swish (light or heavy weapons), bows twang,
  firearms fire, and a hit adds an impact (bludgeoning or slashing). Sounds work with or without JB2A; without Sequencer
  or PSFX nothing is played and no error is shown.</p>
  {{/if}}

  {{#if is.combat}}
  <h2>Combat</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Initiative</strong>: 1d10, lowest first. Rolling one combatant from the tracker asks for its action (weapon
    speed, spell casting time, magical item, natural weapons) and situation; Roll All uses each combatant's weapon in hand.
    The world setting chooses individual, group or standard (one roll per side; sides are token dispositions).</li>
    <li><strong>Attacks</strong>: from the Combat tab (characters) or the Attacks table (monsters). Target tokens first: the
    target's AC fills in, and status icons tick the combat modifiers (prone, stunned, invisible, held or asleep). Two
    weapons, unarmed attacks (punch, wrestle, overbear), mounted combat and missile ranges are options in the dialogs.</li>
    <li><strong>Damage</strong>: rolled automatically on a hit (client setting), or with the Damage button. The GM applies it
    with the buttons under the chat message (to the roll's targets, the GM's targets or the selected tokens) or the message's
    right-click menu. Elemental mages and gens adjust damage of their element there.</li>
    <li><strong>Hit points and death</strong>: the world death rule chooses death at 0 or the "death's door" option
    (unconscious at 0, bleeding each round until wounds are bound, dead at -10). Sheet buttons: Damage, Heal, Rest,
    Bind wounds, Raise. Punching damage and temporary damage return on their own.</li>
    <li><strong>Surprise, morale, reactions</strong>: Surprise on the character sheet and the eye button on monster
    sheets; morale and reaction buttons on monster sheets.</li>
    <li><strong>Modifiers</strong>: every roll dialog has a modifier and reason for anything the system does not track.</li>
    <li><strong>Lasso</strong>: a character or monster attacking with a lasso gets its own dialog: legs (pull/trip), arms (trap),
    unhorse a rider, or a pull/trip by spurring a mount the lasso is tied to. Target the defender first: its Strength,
    Dexterity, THAC0 and size are filled in (for monsters from size, Hit Dice and movement) and can be changed. Tick four
    legs, unaware or stationary as they apply. The thrower's Strength is a field too: a monster's starts at 3.5 per size
    category plus its Hit Dice (change it if you know better). Chat shows every roll and the result.</li>
    <li><strong>Throwing objects</strong>: "Throw object" on a character's Combat tab or a monster's header throws a flask of oil, holy water,
    acid, a rock or another object. Up to 5 lb it reaches 10 / 20 / 30 ft (short, medium, long); for heavier objects you
    decide which band is possible, tick "exceptionally heavy" for a bend bars/lift gates check, and nothing heavier than
    the character's Maximum Press can be thrown. A hit rolls the direct damage to the targets and a splash roll for the
    creatures within 3 ft (apply it with the chat buttons to the tokens you select); flaming oil's second round is a note.
    A miss says where it lands (direction and feet from the target point): move or mark it yourself. The Throwing
    proficiency adds 10 ft to each band (5 more per extra slot) and +1 to hit or damage, for thrown weapons as well.
    Monsters have no Strength score, so their throws have no Maximum Press limit and no bend bars box: decide yourself
    whether the creature can lift the object.</li>
    <li><strong>Net</strong>: attacking with a net (characters and monsters) opens its own dialog: throw (traps weapon and shield; the target AC
    counts only Dexterity and magic, so enter any magical protection), loop the rope round (the victim breaks free at -4),
    pull/trip, or fold the net (2 rounds; after the first throw it is at -4 to hit until folded). A netted character or
    monster uses "Break free (net)" (Combat tab, or the monster's header): a Strength check. A hit marks the first target
    with the "Netted" status icon (looping the rope round also ticks the -4 in its Break free dialog); a successful Break
    free removes it. The mark changes no roll: rule on what a netted creature can do. Remove it by hand from the token's
    status icons if the creature is cut free another way.</li>
    <li><strong>Poison</strong>: a monster's natural attack can carry a DMG Table 51 poison class (Attacks table, Poison
    column); weapons and missiles are coated from their sheet with a poison item (compendium "Poisons (DMG)", one dose per
    weapon or missile); traps have a poison class and how they deliver it. A damage roll with a poisoned attack posts a
    save request vs. poison: each target's owner clicks Save, or you roll them all (dwarves and halflings add their
    Constitution bonus vs. poison). Once a save is in, the system rolls the onset (whispered to you) and applies the
    effect when game time reaches it: damage, death, paralysis (2d6 hours) or debilitation (1d3 days: ability scores and
    movement halved, no healing). Advance game time (the round count in combat does it) for the onset to come. The sheet
    shows pending poison; your "Neutralize poison" button ends it (hit points already lost stay lost). A GM must be
    connected for saves to be recorded. Coated weapons lose their dose on each damage roll (implementation choice).</li>
    <li><strong>Spells, magic resistance and saves</strong>: a caster targets the creatures, then clicks Cast. Each target
    with magic resistance rolls d100 at once (resisted at or below its value; monsters use the first percentage in
    their Magic Resistance text, characters the field next to their saves; "Lowered" skips the roll). No caster-level
    adjustment is applied. If the spell's Save entry is not None, the card asks the remaining targets for a save
    (the category is the spell sheet's "Save vs.", spell by default): owners click Save, or you roll them all. The
    spell's Damage roll then goes to those who failed or did not roll, half to those who saved ("1/2"), nothing for
    "Neg.", and for other entries a separate full-damage message for those who saved, to apply as the spell says.
    Area spells: target every creature in the area.</li>
    <li><strong>Class weapons</strong>: weapons outside the class's list are marked in red and the attack dialog warns;
    the attack is still allowed (your ruling).</li>
  </ul>
  {{/if}}

  {{#if is.monsters}}
  <h2>Monsters, hirelings, mounts and familiars</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Monster / NPC actors</strong> hold a stat block: Hit Dice as written (with HP rolls), THAC0 from the DMG,
    saving throws by Hit Dice, morale, natural attacks plus owned weapons and armour. "Monsters &amp; NPCs (prototypes)"
    has an orc, a riding horse and a mercenary as examples.</li>
    <li><strong>Hirelings &amp; Mounts</strong> compendium: DMG soldiers with their equipment and wages, civilian
    specialists, horses, camels, an elephant, pack animals, familiars, and the Skills &amp; Powers animal companions and
    mounts (Animal Master Table 42, Rider Table 43; see <em>Characters</em>).</li>
    <li><strong>Mounts</strong>: a rider's load is checked against the mount's capacity; the Push button pushes a mount
    beyond its normal pace. With the world setting on, a rider's and mount's tokens move together.</li>
    <li><strong>Familiars</strong>: Find Familiar is rolled by the GM from the character sheet; the familiar's death and
    separation are tracked. Sha'ir characters summon and send their gen from the sheet.</li>
    <li><strong>Importing</strong>: see <em>GM tools</em>. Pictures are not shipped with the system; the importer copies
    them into the world (setting "Store imported monster pictures in the world").</li>
  </ul>
  {{/if}}

  {{#if is.items}}
  <h2>Items, treasure and identification</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Weapons, ammunition, armour, equipment, coins, gems, jewellery and magical items are items on the Inventory tab
    (monsters: Inventory tab). Carried items count toward encumbrance (world setting chooses the encumbrance rule).</li>
    <li><strong>Containers</strong>: drag an item's row onto a container in the Containers list (or choose "In container"
    on the item sheet); contents count while the container is carried, and its capacity is checked.</li>
    <li><strong>Ammunition</strong>: firing a launcher asks which ammunition and spends one.</li>
    <li><strong>Magical items</strong>: "Magical Items (DMG)" lists the DMG tables with XP values and links; wands, rods
    and staves roll their charges; "Use" spends a charge or one potion or scroll.</li>
    <li><strong>Identification</strong>: treasure arrives unidentified. Players see a generic name and no values; only the
    GM ticks Identified on the item sheet. Bonuses still apply.</li>
    <li><strong>Spell components</strong>: with the world setting on, casting a spell uses up its consumed material
    components from the caster's items.</li>
  </ul>
  {{/if}}

  {{#if is.optional}}
  <h2>Optional rules</h2>
  <p>Each is a world setting (see <em>Settings</em>); all are off unless noted.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Called shots and attack options</strong>: attack dialogs get a Maneuver select. Called shot: -4 (or -6/-8
    for hard ones), +1 initiative (announce it before initiative), and with System II criticals the location aimed at.
    Sap: -4 (-8 against a helmet, ticked from the target's equipped helmet), Small or Medium targets; the damage is
    punching damage and a knockout chance (5% per point up to 40%, doubled against a helpless victim) is rolled.
    Disarm, grab, trap and block open a second window with the defender's numbers (filled from the target) and roll both
    sides: opposed attack rolls against AC 0 and AC 4 (block: AC 4 against the opponent's attack on you); a disarmed
    weapon's landing spot is rolled; a grab goes on to opposed Strength. Pull/trip (the weapons the rules name, e.g.
    polearms, spears, bows, whips) and shield-punch / shield-rush (an equipped shield) roll the attack and the Strength
    contest. Monsters get called shots, disarm, grab, trap and block.</li>
    <li><strong>Critical hits (Combat &amp; Tactics)</strong>: Off, System I or System II. A natural 18 or more that hits by
    5 or more is a critical for characters and monsters alike. System I doubles the damage dice (added to other
    multipliers: a charging lance does 3d6). System II also rolls hit location and severity for the chart of the
    weapon's type against the target's kind (characters are humanoids; set a monster's "Critical hit chart" in its
    combat panel, default monster) and posts a save vs. death request: on a failed save open the linked chart for the
    injury and apply it yourself (the injury texts are not copied into the system). Severity 13+ triples the damage
    dice. The Damage dialog shows the critical, pre-set from the attack, so you can change it.</li>
    <li><strong>Weapon type vs. armour (PHB Table 52)</strong>: an attack against a target wearing body armour from the
    table adds its modifier for the weapon's best damage type (a halberd uses slashing or piercing, whichever is
    better). Natural Armor Classes get none. Monster natural attacks need a type (S, P, B) in the Attacks table. The
    modifier shows in the attack's chat line.</li>
    <li><strong>Skills &amp; Powers weapon rules</strong>: weapon groups, fighting styles, armour and shield proficiencies,
    weapon of choice, expertise, specialization for every class and mastery. Character points are not used: costs are
    paid with weapon slots.</li>
    <li><strong>Skills &amp; Powers nonweapon proficiency ratings</strong>: Table 45 ratings with the Table 44 ability
    modifier instead of the PHB check; extra slots add +1, up to 16 before ability modifiers.</li>
    <li><strong>Skills &amp; Powers kits and traits</strong> are always available as items (no setting): the Skills &amp; Powers
    kits folder and the "Traits &amp; Disadvantages" compendium.</li>
    <li><strong>Combat &amp; Tactics weapons</strong> are items in the weapon compendiums (misfires, knockdown dice, firearms
    powder and matches).</li>
    <li><strong>Dual-class</strong> and <strong>multi-class characters</strong>: see <em>Characters</em>.</li>
    <li><strong>Al-Qadim</strong>: kits, sha'ir and gens, elemental mages; the heat penalty for heavy armour is a setting.</li>
    <li><strong>Al-Qadim station</strong>: characters get a Station panel on the Character tab. The player rolls the initial
    station from the Al-Qadim kit; you can type it instead (Initial, in the GM section). Level raises it when higher; a dual-class character's
    initial station is halved. "Spend" pays 1,000 gp a point from the character's coins for a month's bonus. Your buttons (GM section):
    Convict (1d2 replaces the initial station; level still raises it), Penniless (station 3; it ends by itself when the character's coins, gems and jewellery reach
    500 gp per level, unless you tick "Outstanding bills or loans", shown while penniless), Enslave (asks the owner's station) and Free (the station climbs back 1 point a month). Give
    NPC actors a station in their stats panel (pick a Table 1 role and roll, or type it; leave it empty for monsters):
    encounter reaction rolls then add 1 for every 2 full points of difference between the speaker and the creature.</li>
    <li><strong>Spell components</strong>, <strong>maximum spells per level</strong> and the <strong>initiative question</strong>
    are settings too.</li>
  </ul>
  {{/if}}

